*This project has been created as part of the 42 curriculum by aimokhta, azsaleh, jchuah, maxliew.*

# Big2

Big2 is a browser-based multiplayer implementation of Big Two (Big 2). Players create accounts, join a lobby with friends, and play together in real time at a 3D-rendered card table. The project is a TypeScript monorepo of a React client and independently deployable services.

The website is served through Nginx, which routes requests to the authentication, party, profile, friends, chat, and game-stats services. The game server manages lobbies and matches, while the game-bot service connects automated players. Shared TypeScript contracts and PostgreSQL schemas live in `packages/`; database setup is in `infra/`.

## Features

| Feature | What it does | Contributors |
|---|---|---|
| Accounts and sessions | Register, sign in/out, validate sessions, and protect service requests. | Jeremy, Azrul |
| Parties and lobbies | Invite or remove players, set seats, change game settings, and track presence. | Jeremy, Max |
| Big Two gameplay | Play singles and combinations with turn handling, reconnects, timers, and results. | Max |
| Spectating and bots | Watch an active game or fill an open seat with a bot using selectable play styles. | Max (spectating), Jeremy (bots) |
| 3D client | View and interact with a Three.js card table; sort, select, drag, play, or pass cards. | Max, Azrul |
| Chat and emotes | Send party messages and emotes, see typing activity, and receive rate-limit feedback. | Azrul |
| Friends and presence | Send, accept, reject, and remove friend requests; receive live updates and see availability. | Aisyah |
| Profiles and settings | Choose a username and avatar, upload a custom avatar, and configure game preferences. | Jeremy, Azrul |
| Progression and results | View game results, rankings, XP, levels, achievements, badges, and easter eggs. | Aisyah, Max, Azrul |
| Notifications | Receive in-app notices for invitations, activity, and progression. | Azrul, Jeremy |

## Instructions

### Prerequisites

- Docker Engine with Docker Compose v2 (Docker Desktop includes both).
- OpenSSL to generate local secrets and a development TLS certificate.
- Ports `8080`, `3001`, `3002`, and `4983` available; the latter three are for chat testing, game-stats testing, and the local Drizzle Gateway respectively.

The containers use Node.js 20 or 24 and PostgreSQL 17. Root `npm install` is only needed for running workspace scripts outside the Docker setup.

### Configure and run

1. From the repository root, create the local environment file:

   ```sh
   cp .env.example .env
   ```

2. Compose expects password and token files under the ignored `secrets/` directory. Generate local values (keep them private):

   ```sh
   mkdir -p secrets/database-passwords secrets/tokens secrets/ssl
   for name in db-admin-password db-auth-password db-party-password db-profile-password db-friends-password db-game-stats-password; do
     openssl rand -hex 32 > "secrets/database-passwords/$name.txt"
   done
   openssl rand -hex 32 > secrets/tokens/statistics-token.txt
   openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout secrets/ssl/transcendence.key \
     -out secrets/ssl/transcendence.crt -days 365 -subj "/CN=localhost"
   ```

3. Build and start the stack:

   ```sh
   docker compose up --build
   ```

   This starts PostgreSQL, runs database migrations, and then starts the website, API services, game server, bot service, and Nginx. The default `.env.example` config uses HTTP; open [http://localhost:8080](http://localhost:8080). To use HTTPS, set `SSL_ENABLE=ssl` and `SOCKET_PROTOCOL=https` in `.env`, then trust the locally generated certificate in your browser.

4. Stop the stack with `docker compose down` (or `make down`). Persistent database and avatar volumes remain. `make fclean` also removes project volumes and deletes their data.

The website is bind-mounted for Vite hot reload. See the service READMEs under `services/` for API contracts and service-specific testing instructions.

